      "writtenOn": "2024-12-11T04:30:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I tried adding the `deb-src`, then `apt update` failed due to missing Debian archive and signing keys. Even with `debian-archive-keyring` installed, I still needed to import some more Debian signing keys.\n\nAfter a bunch of `gpg --recv-keys` and `apt update`, I could\n\n```\napt-get source linux-image-6.1.0-27-cloud-amd64-unsigned\n```\n\nbut not `6.1.0-28`, because that version is released on the `bookworm-security` channel.\n\nAfter adding this to sources.list:\n\n```\ndeb-src https://security.debian.org/debian-security/ bookworm-security main\n```\n\nand yet again `gpg --recv-keys` for Debian security signing keys, I could finally `apt-get source linux-image-6.1.0-28-cloud-amd64-unsigned`.\n\nIt generates the exact same source tree (because it uses `dpkg-source` internally), so I am not sure if the additional complexity of adding Debian repos and signing keys etc. to make Ubuntu\u0027s apt work nicely is worth the pain.\n\nGoing back to your original questions; if your concern is that `debian-keyring` might be outdated relative to a kernel source package at some point in the future, then I think a simpler alternative would be to `rsync` the Debian keys (as recommended at https://wiki.debian.org/DebianKeyring#Via_rsync) instead of `apt install debian-keyring`.",
      "parentUuid": "695a538c_af900ba4",
